The problem asks for the number of ways to select 6 cookies from 3 types (chocolate chip, oatmeal, peanut butter) where cookies of the same type are indistinguishable and there are at least 6 of each type available. This is equivalent to finding the number of nonnegative integer solutions to \(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 6\), where \(x_1, x_2, x_3\) are the numbers of each type of cookie chosen.

Using stars and bars, the number of solutions is \(\binom{6 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} = \binom{8}{2} = 28\).

\boxed{28}

