The matrix \(\mathbf{M}\) has entries \(\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}\). From the given mappings:

\[
\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 2a - b \\ 2c - d \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 9 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
\implies
\begin{cases}
2a - b = 9 \\
2c - d = 3
\end{cases}
\]

\[
\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} a - 3b \\ c - 3d \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}
\implies
\begin{cases}
a - 3b = 7 \\
c - 3d = -1
\end{cases}
\]

Solving for \(a, b\): \(a = 4,\; b = -1\).  
Solving for \(c, d\): \(c = 2,\; d = 1\).

Thus \(\mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\).

A general point on the line \(y = 2x + 1\) is \(\begin{pmatrix} x \\ 2x + 1 \end{pmatrix}\). Applying \(\mathbf{M}\):

\[
\begin{pmatrix} x' \\ y' \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\ 2x + (2x + 1) \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\ 4x + 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Let \(x = \frac{X + 1}{2}\) from \(X = 2x - 1\). Substituting into \(Y = 4x + 1\):

\[
Y = 4\left(\frac{X + 1}{2}\right) + 1 = 2X + 3
\]

Hence the image of the line is \(y = 2x + 3\).

\[
\boxed{y = 2x + 3}
\]

