The inequality is \(-4 < x^4 + 4x^2 < 21\). Substitute \(y = x^2\) with \(y \ge 0\). The inequality becomes \(-4 < y^2 + 4y < 21\).

The left part, \(y^2 + 4y > -4\), is always true for \(y \ge 0\) because \(y^2 + 4y \ge 0 > -4\). The right part gives \(y^2 + 4y < 21\), or \(y^2 + 4y - 21 < 0\). Factoring yields \((y + 7)(y - 3) < 0\), so \(-7 < y < 3\). With \(y \ge 0\), this becomes \(0 \le y < 3\). Since \(y = x^2\), we have \(x^2 < 3\), which gives \(-\sqrt{3} < x < \sqrt{3}\).

\boxed{(-\sqrt{3}, \sqrt{3})}

