The set \(S\) consists of all points inside the regular nonagon (including its interior) and all points in the plane whose distance to the perimeter of the nonagon is less than 1. The boundary of \(S\) is the set of points exactly 1 unit outward from the perimeter. For a convex polygon, offsetting the boundary outward by a distance \(r\) produces a new boundary made of line segments parallel to the original sides (same total length) and circular arcs of radius \(r\) at the vertices. The total length of the arcs at all vertices sums to a full circle of radius \(r\), adding \(2\pi r\) to the perimeter. 

Here the original nonagon has side length 2 and 9 sides, so its perimeter is \(9 \times 2 = 18\). Offsetting outward by \(r = 1\) adds \(2\pi \times 1 = 2\pi\). The resulting perimeter of \(S\) is \(18 + 2\pi\).

\boxed{18 + 2\pi}

