winner_texts.self_consistency:
The problem involves a circle with center \(Q\) and radius 14 inches. Two perpendicular radii \(AQ\) and \(BQ\) form a quarter-circle sector. Inside this sector, two smaller semicircular regions are drawn with their diameters on the radii \(AQ\) and \(BQ\). These semicircles are tangent to each other and also tangent to the arc of the larger circle. One semicircle has its diameter starting at \(Q\) along \(AQ\) (so its radius is half the big radius, \(7\) inches). The other semicircle has its diameter on \(BQ\) not starting at \(Q\); it is positioned so that it is tangent to the first semicircle and to the big circle. Setting up the tangency conditions yields the radius of the smaller semicircle as \(\frac{1}{3}\) of the large radius.

Thus the radius of the smaller semicircle is \(\frac{14}{3}\) inches.

\boxed{\frac{14}{3}}

