The octagon is equiangular, so all interior angles are \(135^\circ\). Since no two consecutive sides have the same length and there are four sides of length \(1\) and four of length \(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\), the sides must alternate: \(1, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, 1, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \dots\)

Place the polygon in the coordinate plane starting at the origin, with first side along the positive \(x\)-axis and each successive side turning \(45^\circ\) counterclockwise. The vertices become:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&(0,0),\quad (1,0),\quad \left(\tfrac{3}{2},\tfrac{1}{2}\right),\quad \left(\tfrac{3}{2},\tfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad (1,2),\quad (0,2),\quad \left(-\tfrac{1}{2},\tfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad \left(-\tfrac{1}{2},\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
\end{aligned}
\]
Using the shoelace formula on these coordinates gives an area of \(\frac{7}{2}\). Equivalently, the general area formula for an equiangular octagon with alternating side lengths \(a\) and \(b\) is \(a^2 + b^2 + 2\sqrt{2}ab\). Substituting \(a=1\) and \(b=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\) yields \(1 + \frac{1}{2} + 2 = \frac{7}{2}\).

\boxed{\frac{7}{2}}

